          "endLine": 286,
          "code": "        <h3>How to know whether a function returns a Promise</h3>\n        <ol class=\"logic-steps\">\n          <li><strong>Its definition begins with <code>async</code>.</strong><span>An async function always returns a Promise, even when its return statement contains an ordinary string or object.</span></li>\n          <li><strong>Documentation or editor hover shows <code>Promise&lt;Type&gt;</code>.</strong><span><code>Promise&lt;AuthResponse&gt;</code> means the future fulfilled value will be an AuthResponse.</span></li>\n          <li><strong>The returned value supports <code>.then(...)</code>.</strong><span>The expression before <code>.then</code> is a Promise or Promise-like “thenable.”</span></li>\n          <li><strong>The normal usage places <code>await</code> before it.</strong><span>This is a strong clue, although JavaScript technically permits await on ordinary values too.</span></li>\n          <li><strong>Logging the unawaited result shows <code>Promise { &lt;pending&gt; }</code>.</strong><span>This runtime check is useful while learning and debugging.</span></li>\n        </ol>\n        <aside class=\"callout warning\"><strong>Function-call syntax alone is not enough</strong><code>someFunction()</code> could return any type. The definition, type information, documentation, or returned object tells you whether it is a Promise.</aside>\n",
