          "endLine": 768,
          "code": "using (\n  (select auth.jwt() -&gt; 'app_metadata' -&gt;&gt; 'role') = 'admin'\n)</code></pre></div><p>Permissive SELECT policies combine with OR: an employee may pass ownership, while an admin may pass role authorization.</p>\n          <h3>Create the PostgREST relationship</h3><div class=\"code-wrap\"><pre><code>alter table public.logs\n  add constraint logs_user_id_profiles_fkey\n  foreign key (user_id)\n  references public.profiles(id)\n  on delete cascade;</code></pre></div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"module-panel\" id=\"db-logic\" role=\"tabpanel\" hidden><ol class=\"logic-steps\"><li><strong>Migrations run in timestamp order.</strong><span>Later files intentionally modify objects created earlier.</span></li><li><strong>Auth creates or updates a user.</strong><span>The security-definer trigger copies ID, name, and email into profiles.</span></li><li><strong>A browser query reaches Postgres.</strong><span>GRANT first permits the authenticated role to attempt the command.</span></li><li><strong>RLS evaluates applicable policies per row.</strong><span>Ownership or admin JWT claims decide access.</span></li><li><strong>Constraints validate writes.</strong><span>Types, foreign keys, checks, and unique indexes reject invalid states.</span></li><li><strong>The logs/profile foreign key informs PostgREST.</strong><span>Nested <code>profiles(full_name, email)</code> selects become possible after schema reload.</span></li><li><strong>Recurring migrations reshape reminders.</strong><span>Weekdays, time, timezone, and last-sent date replace a one-time-only design.</span></li></ol></div>\n      </section>\n",
